@rem Exercises for lesson 05_operators (C++ track). Format: docs/CONTENT_FORMAT.md

@exercise cpp-op-theory-friend
@type theory
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 2
@prompt
operator<< for cout cannot be a member of your class. Which keyword lets such a free function access the private fields?
@answer
friend
@explain
A friend declaration inside the class gives the free function access to the private members.

@exercise cpp-op-choice-postfix
@type choice
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Which declaration is the POSTFIX increment (c++) of class Clock?
@choices
- Clock& operator++();
* Clock operator++(int);
- Clock operator++(Clock);
- void operator++(c);
@explain
The unnamed int parameter only tells the compiler that this is the postfix version; the prefix version takes no parameter.

@exercise cpp-op-choice-overload
@type choice
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Can two functions in the same scope differ only in their return type?
@choices
- Yes, the compiler looks at the return type
* No, the parameter lists must differ
- Yes, but only for member functions
- Yes, if one is const
@explain
Overload resolution uses the number and types of parameters, not the return type.

@exercise cpp-op-predict-plus
@type predict
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Counter {
public:
    int n;
    Counter(int v) : n(v) { }
    Counter operator+(const Counter& o) const { return Counter(n + o.n); }
};

int main() {
    Counter a(3), b(4);
    Counter c = a + b;
    cout << c.n << " " << a.n << endl;
    return 0;
}
@answer
7 3
@explain
a + b is a.operator+(b): it builds a NEW Counter with 3 + 4. The operands are unchanged.

@exercise cpp-op-predict-prefix-postfix
@type predict
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 3
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Num {
public:
    int v;
    Num(int x) : v(x) { }
    Num& operator++() { ++v; return *this; }
    Num operator++(int) { Num old = *this; ++v; return old; }
};

int main() {
    Num a(5);
    Num b = a++;
    Num c = ++a;
    cout << a.v << " " << b.v << " " << c.v << endl;
    return 0;
}
@answer
7 5 7
@explain
a++ returns the OLD value: b is 5 and a becomes 6. ++a increments first: a becomes 7 and c gets 7.

@exercise cpp-op-fill-operator-plus
@type fill
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Name the member function that makes a + b work for Vec objects.
@code
class Vec {
public:
    int x, y;
    Vec(int a, int b) : x(a), y(b) { }
    Vec ___(const Vec& o) const {
        return Vec(x + o.x, y + o.y);
    }
};
@answer
operator+
@explain
a + b is translated to a.operator+(b).

@exercise cpp-op-fill-prefix-return
@type fill
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Complete the prefix increment. It should return a reference to the object itself.
@code
class Num {
public:
    int v;
    Num(int x) : v(x) { }
    Num& operator++() {
        ++v;
        return ___;
    }
};
@answer
*this
@explain
this is a pointer to the current object, so *this is the object; returning a reference to it is the usual convention.

@exercise cpp-op-fill-postfix-param
@type fill
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Complete the declaration of the postfix increment.
@code
class Num {
public:
    int v;
    Num(int x) : v(x) { }
    Num operator++(___) {
        Num old = *this;
        ++v;
        return old;
    }
};
@answer
int
@explain
The int parameter is unused; it only distinguishes the postfix form from the prefix form.

@exercise cpp-op-predict-overload
@type predict
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this program print? Think about which overload the compiler picks for each call.
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void print(int) { cout << "int "; }
void print(double) { cout << "double "; }
void print(const char*) { cout << "string "; }

int main() {
    print(5);
    print(2.5);
    print("hi");
    print('a');
    cout << endl;
    return 0;
}
@answer
int double string int
@explain
5 is an int, 2.5 a double and "hi" a const char*. A char is promoted to int, so print('a') calls the int version.

@exercise cpp-op-bug-postfix
@type bug
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 2
@prompt
a++ does not compile, because only the prefix form is declared. Click the buggy line, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Num {
public:
    int v;
    Num(int x) : v(x) { }
    Num operator++() {
        Num old = *this;
        ++v;
        return old;
    }
};

int main() {
    Num a(1);
    Num b = a++;
    cout << b.v << " " << a.v << endl;
    return 0;
}
@bugline 8
@answer
Num operator++(int) {
@explain
The postfix form needs the unused int parameter; without it the function is the prefix operator.

@exercise cpp-op-theory-member-params
@type theory
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 2
@prompt
A member operator+ of class Vec is written as  Vec operator+(const Vec& o) const.  How many explicit parameters does it have? Write the number.
@answer
1 | one
@explain
a + b means a.operator+(b): the left operand is the object itself (this), and only the right operand is a parameter.

@exercise cpp-op-theory-ostream
@type theory
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What is the type of the first parameter of  operator<<(..., const Clock& c)  when it is used with cout? Write the type.
@answer
ostream& | ostream & | std::ostream& | std::ostream & | ostream
@explain
cout is an ostream. The function takes it by reference, changes it by writing into it, and returns the same reference so that << can be chained.

@exercise cpp-op-choice-nonmember
@type choice
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Why can operator<< for  cout << myClock  not be a member function of Clock?
@choices
- Because member functions cannot be operators
- Because << may only be overloaded once per program
* Because the left operand is cout, not a Clock, and a member operator always has the object itself on the left
- Because member functions cannot use cout
@explain
A member operator is called on its left operand. Here the left operand is cout, which we cannot change, so the operator has to be a free function (usually a friend).

@exercise cpp-op-bug-operand
@type bug
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 2
@prompt
The program should print 7 but prints 6. Click the buggy line, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Counter {
public:
    int n;
    Counter(int v) : n(v) { }
    Counter operator+(const Counter& o) const {
        return Counter(n + n);
    }
};

int main() {
    Counter a(3), b(4);
    Counter c = a + b;
    cout << c.n << endl;
    return 0;
}
@bugline 9
@answer
return Counter(n + o.n);
@explain
n + n doubles the left operand and ignores the right one. The sum of the two objects is n + o.n.

@exercise cpp-op-bug-return-ref
@type bug
@lesson 05_operators
@difficulty 3
@prompt
This operator+ returns a reference to something that no longer exists. Click the line that is a bug, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Vec {
public:
    int x, y;
    Vec(int a, int b) : x(a), y(b) { }
    Vec& operator+(const Vec& o) const {
        Vec result(x + o.x, y + o.y);
        return result;
    }
};

int main() {
    Vec a(1, 2), b(3, 4);
    Vec c = a + b;
    cout << c.x << " " << c.y << endl;
    return 0;
}
@bugline 8
@answer
Vec operator+(const Vec& o) const {
@explain
result is a local variable and dies when the function returns, so the reference is dangling. A binary operator+ builds a new object and returns it by value.
